
5d-Block Triple-Zeta Basis Set Archive
======================================

Title: Relativistic finite nucleus double-, triple-, and quadruple-zeta
       basis sets for the 5d elements Hf-Hg. Theor. Chem. Acc., submitted

Author: Kenneth G. Dyall

Date: 23 Jun 2003



This archive contains the exponents and the SCF spinor or orbital coefficients, 
given with respect to normalized (spherical or 2-spinor) Gaussian functions, for
the triple-zeta basis sets described in the above article.  The contractions
are taken from SCF calculations on the configuration average of the d^n s^2 and 
d^n+1 s^1 configurations. 

The archive is set out as follows.

1. Exponents and SCF coefficients for Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave functions, in order
   of Z. The large component functions are given first, followed by the small 
   component functions. Both components are labelled with the large component
   symmetry type, but the primitive functions for the small component are
   those obtained by restricted kinetic balance from the large component.
   The spin-orbit components have a "-" label for j=l-1/2 and a "+" label for
   j=l+1/2.

2. Exponents and SCF coefficients for spin-free modified Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave
   functions, in order of Z. The large component functions are given first, 
   followed by the pseudo-large component functions, then the Foldy-Wouthuysen
   transformed large component functions. Note that all components have the same
   symmetry type. 

3. Exponents of valence correlating functions. The f functions are included in
   the SCF set.

4. Exponents of core correlating functions.


Basis set construction.
----------------------

Contracted basis sets can be constructed by adding primitive functions to the SCF 
set. These basis sets are described below. Note that the term "triple zeta" applies 
to the s and p set, and the d set is quadruple zeta. This is a result of keeping a 
reasonable energy balance for the basis sets. Functions are counted in increasing
exponent size, from the smallest.

1. Valence. The valence space is defined here as the 5d and 6s orbitals. To the
   SCF functions, add the 1st and 3rd s primitives, the 1st through 3rd primitives 
   from the p and d sets, the 1st and 2nd f primitives, and the valence correlating 
   g function. 

   (HF + 2s3p3d2f1g valence)

2. Valence+outer core. This set adds the outer-core correlation from the 5s and 5p
   shells. To set 1, add the 4th and 6th s and p primitives, the 4th d primitive
   and the 3rd f primitive.

   If linear dependence problems are encountered in the d space, use one of the 
   following solutions:
   * Delete the 5d orbital, and optionally also delete the 6s orbital and add the 
     2nd s primitive
   * Do not add the 4th d primitive (on the grounds that the 5d orbital will be used 
     to correlate the 5s and 5p). 
   * Add the 5th d primitive instead of the 4th (which results in a loss of about 
     11-16 mEh in the 5s5p correlation energy)
   Note: excitations into the 5d shell were not considered in the determination of the
   exponents to uncontract. 

   (HF + 2s3p3d2f1g valence + 2s2p2d1f outer core; d sets overlap)

3. Valence+outer core+4f.  This set adds functions for correlating the 4f. In addition
   to the core correlating 2g1h set, uncontract the 3rd and 4th f primitives, the 5th
   d primitive, and the 7th and 8th p and s primitives.

   If linear dependence problems are encountered in the d space, omit the 4th d primitive
   or add the 6th and 7th d primitives instead of the 5th. 

   (HF + 2s3p3d2f1g valence + 2s2p2d1f outer core + 2s2p2d2f2g1h core; d sets overlap )

4. Dipole polarization of the 5d. The most diffuse valence correlating f function is 
   tighter than the polarizing function optimized for the double-zeta basis sets. For
   polarization, the valence 2f1g set can be extended by adding an f function in an 
   even-tempered sequence, and a g function using the same ratio as for the f function.
   These additional functions are listed at the end of this archive. 


Dirac-Hartree-Fock basis sets
=============================


 

